In this chapter:

 • A premortem is an obituary you write early enough to falsify. Assume the plan died;
   explain why; fix that, now.
 • Your model will be excellent about supply chains, grids, and legal exposure, and
   silent about the divorce, the burnout, and the shoulder. The room knows what the
   model doesnʼt.
 • Measure a community by one test that matters: can it provide an Adequate Level of
   Care, starting with dinner?
 • Plans are worthless. Planning is everything. The people doing the planning are the
   plan.









